.TH INTRO 9
.SH NAME
intro \- introduction to the kernel
.SH DESCRIPTION
This section documents
.B kernel/
for a developer who changes it.
It assumes
.IR intro (1)
and
.IR intro (2):
what a name space is, what 9P2000.L carries, and what a program expects
of the system it runs under.
Nothing here is user-visible by itself.
A device this code serves is documented in section 3,
a file server built on it in section 4,
and the wire protocol in section 5.
.SS Layout
The kernel is package
.BR kernel ,
built with two import collections,
.B kernel:
bound to the
.B kernel/
directory and
.B vsys:
bound to
.BR sys/ ,
so kernel code reaches the ring-3 libraries as
.BI vsys: name
and its own packages as
.BI kernel: name .
The top level and its packages:
.IP
.EX
kernel/main.odin       kmain: the entry point and boot order, below
kernel/panic.odin      the panic screen
kernel/smp.odin        the other cores; smp(9)
kernel/log.odin        the kernel log, serial and on-screen
kernel/splash.odin     the boot chassis the console and panic screen share
kernel/arch            the architecture interface; arch(9)
kernel/boot/limine     bindings for the Limine boot protocol
kernel/mem             physical memory, page tables, the heap; memory(9)
kernel/sched           threads, priorities, the tick; sched(9)
kernel/sync            spinlocks, sleeping locks, rendezvous; sync(9)
kernel/vfs             the namespace, chans, mount tables, walking; vfs(9)
kernel/mnt             the 9P transport: workers, tags, flush; mnt(9)
kernel/user            the door: syscalls, processes, segments; user(9)
kernel/drivers         hardware drivers: console, fb, kbd, mouse, pci, uart, virtio
kernel/devfs           #c: the console device and its raw taps
kernel/env             #e: the environment device
kernel/procfs          #p: the process table device
kernel/srv             #s: posted services
kernel/sd              #S: storage devices
kernel/ether           #E: the network card
kernel/pipe            the kernel-resident pipe every posted service uses
.EE
.PP
.B kernel/mem/space.odin
and
.B kernel/user/segment.odin
are an address space per process; see
.IR space (9).
The
.B verify_*.odin
files at the top level and one
.B verify.odin
per package are the self-tests
.I kmain
runs at boot; see
.IR testing (9).
.SS Boot order
.I Kmain
in
.B kernel/main.odin
runs once, on the boot core, in this order:
.IP \(bu
.B arch.early_init
turns on the vector unit before any Odin statement can touch a struct,
then the Odin runtime starts and
.B context
becomes usable.
.IP \(bu
the serial port, so a log line reaches somewhere even if every later
step fails.
.IP \(bu
the base revision the bootloader granted is checked against the one asked
for.
.IP \(bu
the framebuffer and the console inside it, if the bootloader handed one
over.
.IP \(bu
a survey of what the bootloader reported: memory map, CPU count, paging
mode, kernel layout.
.PP
From there, each stage depends on the ones before it and is skipped, with
a logged reason, if its prerequisite failed: physical memory and paging
.RI ( memory (9)),
the Vectra9 codec self-test, the root namespace
.RI ( vfs (9)),
the scheduler
.RI ( sched (9)),
the timer and preemption, the device servers
.RB ( #c ", " #e ", " #p ", " #s ),
the pipe layer, the baked-in programs, the keyboard and mouse, the disk
and its file systems, ring 3
.RI ( user (9)),
and last the other cores
.RI ( smp (9)).
Every self-test above a stage runs before that stage can change what it
counts.
The last thing
.I kmain
does is start
.B /bin/rc /lib/init
on the console; see
.IR init (8).
A stage that finds no hardware to bring up degrades and logs a warning
rather than stopping the boot.
.SS Three rules
Three rules recur through the kernel and are not restated in every page
that depends on them.
.PP
A decoded 9P message borrows its buffer.
.I Decode
in
.B sys/vectra9
never copies a string or a byte slice out of the wire buffer it read;
every
.B Msg
field points into that buffer.
The caller keeps the buffer alive for as long as it keeps the
.BR Msg ,
and a reply built from borrowed fields is only as good as the storage it
points into.
See
.IR vectra9 (2)
and
.IR mnt (9).
.PP
A sleeping lock is never taken inside a spinlock.
.B sync.can_sleep
checks two things: the per-core count of spinlocks currently held, and
whether the caller is a top half that cannot be the thread put to sleep.
A thread holding a
.B Spinlock
has interrupts masked, so if it leaves the CPU nothing turns them back
on.
.I Rpc
in
.B kernel/vfs
calls
.B sync.can_sleep
before it sends a message that might park, and refuses with
.B EDEADLK
rather than risk it.
See
.IR sync (9).
.PP
A self-test that cannot fail proves nothing.
Every
.B verify.odin
runs on the real machine, exercises the actual code path a caller uses,
and is written so that removing the fix it was written for turns it red.
See
.IR testing (9).
.SS Toolchain constraints
These bind kernel code specifically, past what
.IR build (8)
says about the toolchain generally.
.IP \(bu
The build passes
.B -no-thread-local
throughout.
Odin's thread-local variables emit
.B STT_TLS
symbols with no
.B PT_TLS
segment in a freestanding image, and
.B ld.lld
refuses to link one.
Per-CPU state goes through a segment register, not a thread-local
variable.
.IP \(bu
Linking uses
.BR ld.lld ,
because the host's system linker cannot produce an ELF image.
.IP \(bu
A bootloader request not placed in the
.B .limine_requests
link section is silently ignored.
Since base revision 2 the section's delimiters are binding, not
advisory: the request still compiles and links, and its
.B response
field stays nil forever.
.IP \(bu
.B EFER.NXE
must be set before the first mapping with the no-execute bit is
installed.
Bit 63 of a page table entry is reserved, not ignored, until then, and
a mapping made first faults on first touch as a reserved-bit page
fault, far from the code that set the bit.
.IP \(bu
The kernel's segment bounds
.RI ( __text_start
through
.IR __data_end )
come from
.B kernel/link_amd64.ld
through a bare
.B foreign
block, not from Odin.
They are declared inside their output sections deliberately: declared
between sections they become orphans, and the linker is free to attach
an orphan section to whichever segment it likes.
.IP \(bu
Inline
.B asm
is a template checked against the compiler's encoding tables, Intel
operand order, one physical register or memory operand per slot.
An instruction with no operand form in the checker
.RB ( in ", " out ", " hlt ", " syscall )
is written as a
.B #byte
sequence instead.
Code that defines a symbol, takes its own label's address, or is
entered directly by the CPU is a
.B .S
file, assembled by clang, not an
.B asm
block.
.IP \(bu
The vector list of which interrupts push an error code is written
twice: once as an assembler
.B .if
in
.B isr.S
and once as
.B vector_has_error_code
in
.BR idt.odin .
The two cannot share a definition, one is read at build time and the
other at run time, and if they disagree every field past the mismatch
in
.B Trap_Frame
reads from the wrong offset.
.IP \(bu
An unoptimised build spills every temporary to the stack.
Code that must observe live register state, such as an FXSAVE check,
has to pin the value with inline asm and hold it there, or a debug
build's own spilling makes the check pass regardless of the bug.
.IP \(bu
A missing end-of-interrupt on the local APIC delivers nothing further
at or below that priority, with no fault and no status bit.
It looks exactly like a timer that was never armed.
Any loop that waits on the tick count needs a liveness bound.
.IP \(bu
A freed slab object still reads as a valid one, because the free-list
link overwrites only the first field.
A structure freed one reference early reports the same zeroed members
a correctly dissolved one does, so a lifetime bug is tested by
checking a reference count, not by inspecting a freed block's fields.
.IP \(bu
The local APIC coalesces ticks it cannot deliver while interrupts are
masked.
Code that assumes a preemption rate proportional to timer frequency has
to account for how much of the time interrupts are actually enabled.
.IP \(bu
Every procedure entered by assembly or by the bootloader is declared
.BR "proc ""c""" ,
never
.BR "proc ""sysv""" ,
which the compiler accepts only on amd64.
.IP \(bu
On the arm64 and riscv64 ports, an inline
.B asm
template has no operand form for a system instruction
.RB ( "msr daifset" ,
the barriers,
.BR tlbi ,
.BR brk ,
.BR svc ,
.BR ecall ,
.BR ebreak ,
.BR sfence.vma ),
so each is a
.B #byte
sequence with its register pinned to
.B x0
or
.BR a0 .
See
.IR ports (9).
.IP \(bu
riscv64 links pass
.BR "-z norelro" ,
because
.B ld.lld
otherwise carves a read-only segment for the GOT out of
.B .data
and starts
.B .bss
mid-page, which the build refuses.
.IP \(bu
riscv64 boots QEMU's
.B virt
board with
.BR acpi=off .
The clock rate is a device tree property and nothing else states it, so
the port reads the tree rather than ACPI.
